[c0002] **What changed** — `src/level/layout/mod.rs`:
[c0002] - `cell_to_world(col, row, cols, gap)` is now the only place a block's world position comes from its grid coordinates. `generate_block_grid` and `interpret_grid` both call it and compute no origin, step or centring themselves. `interpret_grid` counts columns as it walks a line instead of carrying an x cursor; its first-line column count and the "slots under two chars don't advance" quirk are untouched (that's `c0008`'s job).
[c0002] - `world_to_cell(pos, cols, gap)` inverts it, snapping to the nearest cell centre so the gap belongs to the nearer cell. It's dead code until `c0006` — one more `never used` warning alongside the ~20 already in the build.
[c0002] **The one judgement call worth knowing about**: I kept the step accumulation (a loop) rather than `y0 + row * y_step`. The two loops this replaces accumulated, and multiplying disagrees with that by an ULP or two from row 2 up — ~1e-5 of drift nobody would see, but the card asked for byte-identical positions, and stepping is cheap enough to actually mean it. There's a comment on the function saying so, since it otherwise looks like something to "optimise".
[c0002] Two tests pin the output against copies of the pre-refactor loops (filled grids 1..=12 cols × 0..=8 rows, plus layouts shaped like the shipped levels). I checked they're not vacuous: swapping the stepping for a multiply fails both, and I restored the file afterwards.
[c0002] Also note `world_to_cell` keeps the signature the card names, so it bounds columns and rows-below-zero but is open upwards — `c0006` has the row count to hand and can reject rows above the grid itself. That's recorded in the card's Notes in case you'd rather it took `rows`.
